Z||000004||bad911||07-12-2001||12:19 PM||||Welcome! A few of us are doing 4.5's and 4.9's. Bubbajoe is the guru.<P>Call me old and lazy but my stuff has auto, so I'm hanging with the Caddy slush-boxes.<P>------------------<BR>87 2.8 AT SE<BR>85 4.9 project<BR>84 4.5 project||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||07-12-2001||04:18 PM||||Here's one of mine <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/cadero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/cadero.htm</A> <P>What year is that 4.5 you're looking at? If it's a '90, be prepared to convert it to carb or TBI, or do a lot of messing around to get the PFI to work.||||reg||14
Z||000006||ichifiero||07-12-2001||06:48 PM||||The year of the Caddy is '89.<BR>Is it reasonble to use the caddy trans?||||reg||1
Z||000007||PBJ||07-12-2001||08:55 PM||||If I were you I would stick with the trans that is already in the fiero.  If it is a stick than I think it would be cheaper to get a 88 2.8 fiero (neutral balanced) flywheel plugged and redrilled like bubbajoe suggests than it would be to swap in an auto.  If you already have an auto it will bolt right up torque converter and all and work well.  so I have been told!!<P>Keep us informed ehh.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by PBJ (edited 07-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||ichifiero||07-13-2001||12:09 AM||||The problem with my current auto trans is that it is shot and would need to be replaced anyway. I was thinking that a 4 speed would give me better acceleration and better fuel milage.||||reg||7
Z||000009||KRMFiero||07-13-2001||06:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ichifiero:<BR><B>The problem with my current auto trans is that it is shot and would need to be replaced anyway. I was thinking that a 4 speed would give me better acceleration and better fuel milage.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I belive that the Caddy Trans is geared alot higher so you will have worst acceleration but you will get great fuel milage, now if you keep the Caddy engine with its Trans since it has alot of power it will have better acceleration then your old engine/trans and probley the same fuel milage  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfixit58||07-13-2001||07:47 AM||||There are a couple of transmission experts on the list (and I'm not one of them) but I believe your Caddy's tranny is almost a bolt in conversion. You may have to swap a couple of axles but I think they are from factory GM cars. <P>As far as the gear ratio, usually a tranny with an OD will have a LOWER first gear ratio which would mean better acceleration and better gas mileage when in OD. The best thing to do would be find out which model of tranny you have and then start asking questions. I'd be willing to bet that someone here on this list has the answer.<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||3
Z||000011||LAMBO||07-13-2001||11:53 AM||||Use the Caddy tranny. All you need are tranny mounts from a Pontiac 6000. Direct bolt in, no axel mods. This is an excellent swap. You've got the right set up (TBI). You just need bubbajoe's front motor mounts and you're almost there. His website has all the info you need to do this swap. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Todd||||reg||1
Z||000012||Alex4mula||07-13-2001||02:09 PM||||Are the 4.9 TBI also? Is this 4.5 swap easier than a 4.9?<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red & White (:<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||PBJ||07-13-2001||02:23 PM||||Yes 4.5 is easier than a 4.9 due to pcm ecm concerns.  you could get a 4.9 and put a 4.5 intake and tbi.  But overall a 4.5 has good horse power and great torque for the ease of the conversion.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Alex4mula||07-13-2001||03:15 PM||||Thanks PBJ. Do you know the diff. in HP/TQ between the two?<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red & White (:<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||rockcrawl||07-13-2001||03:41 PM||||'90 was the first year for PFI, and the only year for the 4.5 PFI. The TBI intake will fit a PFI engine, but it isn't the best thing for performance. The PFI engines had bigger valves and ports. A TBI intake on a PFI is like breathing through a strwaw. Take a look at the throttle bores of each, there's quite a difference. Also note the power difference. Anyway, your engine is already a TBI, so nothing to worry about. <P>The Caddy four speed is so easy to swap in, it'd almost be stupid not to. You don't need mounts from any other car, all the peices you need come from the Caddy and the Fiero and they basicly bolt in. You will need different axles.||||reg||7
Z||000016||mrfixit58||07-13-2001||04:22 PM||||See... I told you there were some experts here.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Thanks for backing me up guys.<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||14
Z||000017||KRMFiero||07-13-2001||04:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LAMBO:<BR><B>Use the Caddy tranny. All you need are tranny mounts from a Pontiac 6000. Direct bolt in, no axel mods. <BR>Todd</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The Trans mounts HAVE TO BE 1989 Ponitac 6000 2.8 Automatic /W Overdrive. The Axles will be streched a little bit, will not affect it though.<P><BR>Does anyone know what axles out of what car is a exact fit (if there is any)||||reg||1
Z||000018||rockcrawl||07-13-2001||05:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>The Trans mounts HAVE TO BE 1989 Ponitac 6000 2.8 Automatic /W Overdrive<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Not so! I don't consider myself an expert, but I have done a few of these swaps, and never used parts from a 6000. In fact all of the 4t60s I've seen use the same brackets, and the brackets will work with the fiero rubber mounts. A few of the ones that will work include '87 Olds Delta 88, '87 Cadillac Deville, '90 Cadillac Deville, 93 Buick Park Ave, '89 Cadillac Seville. Around '91 GM switched to a one piece front mount. This mount replaces the two pieces used previously, and it fits the 4T60 or 4T60E in a Fiero with no mods. <P>The Axles from a 6000/Ciera with 4spd and light duty brakes will fit. Not sure of the year span, but '89 is in there. They are nearly impossible to find with LD brakes, but you can order the axles from auto parts. Or you can use the axles from a car with HD brakes and replace the outer joints with those from a manual Fiero. If you're ordering new axles anyway, specify LD brakes and you won't need to do anything to them. This has been covered many times before on the forum.<P>FYI, the shift cable bracket and arm need to be modified. Check my website for pictures in the Cadero section.||||reg||1
Z||000019||PBJ||07-13-2001||08:27 PM||||I found this very usefull for info. <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/motorcity/track/2582/cadillac/cadindex.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/motorcity/track/2582/cadillac/cadindex.html</A> <P>DeVille 89-93  after all the pictures is engine specs and more.<BR>ps. the 4.6 is the northstar<BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/petesigsm.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by PBJ (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||1
